.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1149 PM PDT Sat Oct 31 2026
(Tonight through Monday)

Popcorn shower activity focused over the interior CEntral Coast
earlier in the nigh has since faded or moved eastward into the
Central.

Focus quickly turns westward as a low pressure is now entering the
northern coastal waters. The low pressure system and associated
frontal boundaries are producing showers and isolated
thunderstorms. KMUX radar even picked up some weak rotation with
the stronger cells, but they`re pulsing on any rotation.
Environmental parameters show less instability and shear are
present the closer one gets toward the coastline. As such, expect
the shower activity to hold together into Sunday mid- morning,
but not expecting any thunderstorms over land. By Sunday afternoon
any showers will end with increasing sunshine. Rainfall amounts
will be a trace to 0.25". Winds increase by the low pressure/cold
front with breezy conditions at the coast.

Coastal flooding remains possible along the bayshore locations of
the San Francisco, San Pablo, and Monterey Bays through Sunday at
6 PM PST.

Tonight through Monday - Winds ease overnight with clearing
skies. Given the recent rain, decreasing winds, and clearing skies
patchy fog is possible Monday morning. Warmer and drier
conditions to kick off the work week.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1222 AM PDT Sun Nov 1 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

Upper level ridge moves eastward as another upper level low/trough
takes aim at the Bay Area Tuesday. As such, the next round of
rain, albeit light, will move Tuesday and Tuesday night. Rainfall
amounts with this system will be greatest over the North Bay with
amounts 0.10-0.30", Bay Area is less than 0.10", and Central
Coast is trace to nothing.

Thereafter, zonal flow to ridging develops by Wednesday and
continues into next weekend. If the forecast holds, temperatures
will return to the 70s by Friday possibly a few low 80s interior
on Saturday.

